@startuml race-condition-without-election
title US-07: Race Condition Without Leader Election - Why Naive Dedup Fails

skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam participantBorderColor #555555
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #F8F8F8
skinparam noteBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7

participant "Evaluator\nInstance A" as A
participant "Evaluator\nInstance B" as B
database "Redis\nalert-sent:{event_id}" as Redis
queue "Kafka\nalerts" as Kafka
actor Operator

note over A, B
  Naive approach: check a Redis flag
  before emitting. No leader election.
  Both instances run evaluation simultaneously.
end note

A -> Redis : GET alert-sent:event-123
B -> Redis : GET alert-sent:event-123

Redis --> A : nil (not sent yet)
Redis --> B : nil (not sent yet)

note over A, B
  Both instances see "not sent"
  at the same moment.
  Race condition: both proceed to emit.
end note

A -> Redis : SET alert-sent:event-123
B -> Redis : SET alert-sent:event-123

A -> Kafka : publish alert for event-123
B -> Kafka : publish alert for event-123

Kafka --> Operator : duplicate alert #1
Kafka --> Operator : duplicate alert #2

note over Operator
  Operator receives two alerts
  for the same event.
  The check-then-act pattern
  is not atomic - it cannot prevent
  this race condition.
  Leader election eliminates the race
  by ensuring only one instance
  ever evaluates at a time.
end note

@enduml
